            ['EPR targets and certificates', '<p>Each year, a PIBO’s target is set as a share of the plastic packaging it put on the market, separately for each category. The obligations cover recycling, reuse for rigid packaging, minimum use of recycled plastic content and, for plastic that cannot be recycled, end-of-life disposal.</p><p>PIBOs meet recycling targets by buying <strong>EPR certificates</strong> generated on the portal by registered plastic waste processors, which are transferred to them on the portal. Not every certificate counts towards every target; see <a href="/news-item/major-alert-india-tightens-epr-rules-end-of-life-eol-certificates-no-longer-valid-for-recycling-targets">why end-of-life certificates no longer count towards recycling targets</a>.</p>'],
            ['Penalties for non-compliance', '<p>CPCB levies <strong>Environmental Compensation</strong> on PIBOs that miss their targets, calculated on the shortfall. Part of it can be returned if the shortfall is made up in later years, as set out in the guidelines. Operating without registration, false declarations or missed annual returns can also lead to compensation, suspension or cancellation of registration, and action under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986. Check the <a href="/resources/blogs/epr-compliance-deadline-2026">EPR compliance deadlines for 2026</a>.</p>'],
