        'description' => 'ANSI/ESD S20.20 certification for electronics makers: programme requirements, EPA setup, staff training, audit process, timeline and cost.',
        'lead' => 'ANSI/ESD S20.20 is the ESD Association’s standard for an electrostatic discharge (ESD) control programme. Electronics, semiconductor, automotive, aerospace and defence customers ask their suppliers for it, and certification is issued by certification bodies approved under the ESD Association’s facility certification programme.',
        'sections' => [
            ['ANSI/ESD S20.20 requirements', '<p>The standard has administrative and technical requirements:</p><ul>
<li><strong>ESD control programme plan:</strong> the scope, the sensitivity of the items handled and who is responsible.</li>
<li><strong>Training plan</strong> and <strong>compliance verification plan</strong>, with records.</li>
<li><strong>Grounding and bonding</strong> of work surfaces, equipment and people.</li>
<li><strong>Personnel grounding:</strong> wrist straps for seated work, and footwear with flooring for people who move around.</li>
<li><strong>ESD protected areas (EPAs)</strong> with marked boundaries, where only protected handling is allowed.</li>
<li><strong>Insulators and isolated conductors</strong> kept away from sensitive items or neutralised with ionisers.</li>
<li><strong>Packaging and marking</strong> that protect sensitive items in storage and transport.</li>
<li><strong>Product qualification</strong> of the ESD control items used.</li>
</ul><p>Our <a href="/resources/blogs/ansi-esd-s20-20-certification-requirements">guide to ANSI/ESD S20.20 requirements</a> covers each of them.</p>'],
            ['ESD training: initial and recurrent', '<p>S20.20 requires <strong>initial training</strong> for everyone who handles or works near ESD-sensitive items, before they do so, and <strong>recurrent training</strong> at intervals set in your training plan. Training records must be kept, and the plan must say how you check that people have understood it, usually with a test.</p><p>Training should cover why ESD matters, the EPA rules, grounding and its daily checks, handling and packaging. Contractors, visitors and maintenance staff who enter the EPA need training or an escort.</p>'],
            ['ESD S20.20 audit process', '<ol>
<li><strong>Gap assessment</strong> against S20.20 and a check of the facility’s ESD controls.</li>
<li><strong>Programme set-up:</strong> the plans, EPA layout, grounding, training and verification records.</li>
<li><strong>Internal audit</strong> and corrective actions.</li>
<li><strong>Certification audit</strong> by an approved body: a review of the documents and an on-site audit with measurements of the ESD controls.</li>
<li><strong>Closure of nonconformities</strong> and issue of the certificate.</li>
<li><strong>Surveillance audits</strong> during the certificate cycle and <strong>recertification</strong> at the end of it.</li>
</ol>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is ANSI/ESD S20.20?', 'It is the ESD Association’s standard for developing an electrostatic discharge control programme to protect electronic parts, assemblies and equipment. It is harmonised with IEC 61340-5-1, and facilities can be certified to it by approved certification bodies.'],
            ['Is ESD training mandatory?', 'Under S20.20, yes. Everyone who handles ESD-sensitive items must have initial training before doing so and recurrent training at the intervals in the training plan, with records kept.'],
