        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'certification/americas/fcc-certification',
        'title' => 'FCC Certification from India: Cost, Process & Timeline',
        'description' => 'FCC certification for Indian manufacturers: SDoC vs certification, FCC ID, TCB grant, cost and timeline, plus what "wait for FCC authorization" means.',
        'lead' => 'FCC certification is required for electronic and radio products sold in the USA. Products with radios, such as Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and cellular devices, need an FCC ID granted by a Telecommunication Certification Body (TCB); most other digital devices are approved through the supplier’s own Declaration of Conformity (SDoC).',
        'sections' => [
            ['SDoC vs FCC certification', '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Supplier’s Declaration of Conformity (SDoC)</th><th>Certification</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Typical products</td><td>Digital devices without radios, such as PCs, monitors, peripherals, chargers and LED drivers</td><td>Intentional radiators, such as Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, cellular, RFID and wireless chargers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing</td><td>EMC tests to FCC Part 15 (and other parts that apply)</td><td>Tests at an FCC-recognised accredited lab</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who approves</td><td>The responsible party, which must be located in the USA</td><td>A TCB, which issues a grant</td></tr>
<tr><td>FCC ID</td><td>Not needed</td><td>Required on the product or its e-label</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public listing</td><td>No</td><td>Grant listed in the FCC equipment authorisation database</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>A product with a radio module and other digital circuits can need both: certification for the radio and SDoC for the rest.</p>'],
            ['FCC ID and TCB grant', '<p>An FCC ID has two parts: the <strong>grantee code</strong>, assigned once to your company by the FCC, and a <strong>product code</strong> you choose for each device. To get a grantee code, you first register for an FCC Registration Number (FRN).</p><p>The lab’s test reports, photos, user manual, label, block diagram and schematics are submitted to a TCB. The TCB reviews them and issues the <strong>grant of equipment authorisation</strong>, which is then published in the FCC database under your FCC ID. Read our <a href="/resources/blogs/fcc-certification-cost-india-requirements">FCC certification cost guide for Indian manufacturers</a>.</p>'],
            ['What does “wait for FCC authorization” mean?', '<p>The phrase means a product, or a radio feature in it, has <strong>not yet been authorised by the FCC</strong>. Under FCC rules, a device that needs authorisation may be advertised or shown before the grant only with a notice that it has not been authorised and cannot be sold or leased until it is, and it may not be delivered to buyers until it is authorised.</p><ul>
<li><strong>If you see it on a listing or a pre-order:</strong> the seller is waiting for the grant; delivery should follow once it is issued.</li>
<li><strong>If you see it on a device:</strong> some makers keep a radio feature disabled until the authorisation covers it, and enable it later through a firmware update.</li>
<li><strong>If you are the manufacturer:</strong> do not ship to US customers until the grant (or your SDoC) is in place.</li>
</ul>'],
