        'faqs' => [
            ['What is the ICDR portal?', 'ICDR (Indian Counterfeited Device Restriction), now called Device Setu, is the Department of Telecommunications portal where manufacturers, importers and brand owners register device IMEIs and generate IMEI certificates.'],
            ['Is an IMEI certificate needed for customs?', 'Yes. Imported devices with IMEIs need a certificate from the ICDR portal, and customs verifies each consignment against it. Without it, the shipment can be held.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'post',
        'key' => 'what-is-rohs',
        'title' => 'What Is RoHS? Restricted Substances, Limits & Compliance',
        'description' => 'RoHS explained: the 10 restricted substances and limits, EU RoHS vs India E-Waste RoHS, exemptions, testing, and how to prove your product is RoHS compliant.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>RoHS (Restriction of Hazardous Substances) limits 10 substances, including lead, mercury and cadmium, in electrical and electronic products.</strong> Each substance has a maximum concentration in every homogeneous material of the product: 0.1% by weight, or 0.01% for cadmium.</p>',
        'top' => [
            ['The 10 RoHS substances and limits', '<table><thead><tr><th>Substance</th><th>Maximum concentration</th><th>Typical uses</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Lead (Pb)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Solder, glass, PVC stabilisers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mercury (Hg)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Lamps, switches, sensors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cadmium (Cd)</td><td>0.01%</td><td>Pigments, plating, batteries</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hexavalent chromium (Cr VI)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Metal coatings and passivation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Polybrominated biphenyls (PBB)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Flame retardants</td></tr>
<tr><td>Polybrominated diphenyl ethers (PBDE)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Flame retardants</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bis(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate (DEHP)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Plasticiser in cables and PVC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Butyl benzyl phthalate (BBP)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Plasticiser</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dibutyl phthalate (DBP)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Plasticiser</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diisobutyl phthalate (DIBP)</td><td>0.1%</td><td>Plasticiser</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Limits are by weight in each homogeneous material, not in the whole product. The four phthalates were added by Delegated Directive (EU) 2015/863.</p>'],
        ],
        'before' => 'FAQs: Your RoHS Questions Answered',
        'sections' => [
            ['EU RoHS vs India RoHS', '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>EU RoHS</th><th>India RoHS</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Law</td><td>Directive 2011/65/EU, as amended</td><td>E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022</td></tr>
<tr><td>Substances</td><td>10, including four phthalates</td><td>6: lead, mercury, cadmium, hexavalent chromium, PBB and PBDE</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limits</td><td>0.1% (cadmium 0.01%)</td><td>0.1% (cadmium 0.01%)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Products</td><td>Almost all electrical and electronic equipment</td><td>Equipment listed in the e-waste rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proof</td><td>EU declaration of conformity, technical file and CE marking</td><td>RoHS compliance declaration by the producer, with CPCB sample checks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exemptions</td><td>Listed in Annexes III and IV</td><td>Listed in the e-waste rules</td></tr>
</tbody></table>'],
            ['What makes a product RoHS compliant?', '<p>A product is RoHS compliant when no homogeneous material in it exceeds the limits, unless an exemption applies, and the maker can prove it. That proof usually combines:</p><ul>
<li>supplier declarations and material data for each part;</li>
<li>test reports for higher-risk materials, such as solder, cables, plastics and coatings;</li>
<li>a technical file and a signed declaration of conformity; and</li>
<li>controls that keep it that way when suppliers or materials change.</li>
</ul>'],
            ['RoHS testing process', '<ol>
<li><strong>Disassembly</strong> of the product into homogeneous materials.</li>
<li><strong>XRF screening</strong> to find materials that may contain restricted elements.</li>
<li><strong>Confirmatory wet chemistry</strong> under the IEC 62321 series: ICP for lead, mercury and cadmium; a colorimetric test for hexavalent chromium; GC-MS for PBB, PBDE and the phthalates.</li>
<li><strong>Report</strong> with results for each material against the limits.</li>
</ol><p>We arrange this at accredited labs through our <a href="/services/testing/rohs-testing">RoHS testing service</a>; see also <a href="/resources/blogs/fast-iso-17025-accredited-rohs-testing">ISO 17025-accredited RoHS testing</a>.</p>'],
            ['RoHS declaration template', '<p>Adapt this to your product and the market it is for:</p><blockquote><p><strong>RoHS Declaration of Conformity</strong></p><ol>
<li>Manufacturer: name and address.</li>
<li>Product: name, model numbers and brand.</li>
<li>We declare under our sole responsibility that the product above complies with [Directive 2011/65/EU as amended by (EU) 2015/863 / the RoHS provisions of the E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022].</li>
<li>Applicable exemptions, if any: [exemption numbers].</li>
<li>Supporting evidence: [test report numbers and dates, supplier declarations].</li>
<li>Signed for and on behalf of the manufacturer: [name, position, place, date, signature].</li>
</ol></blockquote>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What does RoHS compliant mean?', 'It means no homogeneous material in the product contains more of the restricted substances than allowed: 0.1% by weight, or 0.01% for cadmium, unless an exemption applies. The maker backs this with a declaration, a technical file and test reports or supplier data.'],
            ['Is RoHS mandatory in India?', 'Yes, for electrical and electronic equipment covered by the E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022. Producers must keep lead, mercury, cadmium, hexavalent chromium, PBB and PBDE within the limits, and CPCB checks samples from the market.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/indian-registration-compliances/business-licenses/psara-registration',
        'title' => 'PSARA Licence: Fees, Documents & Process (State-wise)',
        'description' => 'How to get a PSARA licence for a private security agency: eligibility, state-wise fees, documents, training tie-up, police verification and timeline.',
        'lead' => 'A PSARA licence is mandatory to run a private security agency in India. It is issued under the Private Security Agencies (Regulation) Act, 2005 by the Controlling Authority of each state, for one district, several districts or the whole state.',
        'sections' => [
            ['Who can apply: eligibility', '<ul>
<li>The agency must be registered in India as a company, firm, LLP or proprietorship, and its proprietor, majority shareholder, partners or directors must meet the Act’s citizenship conditions.</li>
<li>None of them should have been convicted of an offence that bars them, dismissed from government service for misconduct, or linked to a banned organisation.</li>
<li>The agency must arrange training for its guards and supervisors, usually through a tie-up (MoU) with a recognised training institute.</li>
<li>Guards must meet the Act’s conditions on age, physical fitness and police verification.</li>
</ul>'],
            ['PSARA licence fees', '<table><thead><tr><th>Area of operation</th><th>Licence fee</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>One district</td><td>₹5,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>More than one and up to five districts</td><td>₹10,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Whole state</td><td>₹25,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>These are the fees under the PSAR Act, 2005. Under the Central Model Rules, 2020, the renewal fee is the same as the fee for a new licence. Some states add their own charges, so check your state’s rules. An agency working in more than one state needs a licence in each state.</p>'],
            ['Documents required for a PSARA licence', '<ul>
<li>Application in Form I, with Form II (antecedents) for every proprietor, majority shareholder, partner or director</li>
<li>Affidavit in Form III</li>
<li>Certificate of incorporation, partnership deed or other registration, with MoA and AoA for companies</li>
<li>PAN and GST registration</li>
<li>Proof of the office address</li>
<li>MoU with a recognised training institute</li>
<li>ID, address proof and photographs of the proprietor, partners or directors</li>
<li>EPF and ESIC registration, where the state asks for them</li>
</ul>'],
            ['PSARA application process', '<ol>
<li>Choose the area of operation: one district, up to five districts or the whole state.</li>
<li>Arrange the training tie-up and prepare the documents.</li>
<li>Apply to the state Controlling Authority, online where the state has a portal, and pay the fee.</li>
<li>The Controlling Authority sends the antecedents for <strong>police verification</strong> and makes its own enquiries.</li>
<li>After the police no-objection, the licence is granted, or refused with reasons after a hearing.</li>
</ol>'],
            ['How long PSARA takes: timeline', '<p>Under the Act, the Controlling Authority should decide within <strong>60 days</strong> of a complete application and the police no-objection. In practice, police verification of every director or partner takes the longest, so plan for two to three months in most states.</p>'],
            ['PSARA licence validity and renewal', '<p>A PSARA licence is valid for <strong>five years</strong>. Apply for renewal <strong>at least 45 days before expiry</strong> under the Central Model Rules; some states, such as Delhi, require 90 days. A late renewal is not processed, and the agency is treated as unlicensed after expiry and must apply afresh. The renewed licence runs for five years from the expiry of the previous one.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is the PSARA licence fee?', 'Under the PSAR Act, 2005 the fee is ₹5,000 for one district, ₹10,000 for more than one and up to five districts, and ₹25,000 for the whole state. Some states add their own charges.'],
            ['How do I apply for a PSARA licence?', 'Apply to your state’s Controlling Authority in Form I with Form II for each director or partner, a Form III affidavit, company documents and a training institute tie-up, and pay the fee. The licence is issued after police verification.'],
            ['How long does PSARA take?', 'The Act gives the Controlling Authority 60 days from a complete application and police no-objection. In practice it often takes two to three months, mainly for police verification.'],
            ['How long is a PSARA licence valid?', 'Five years. Renewal must be applied for at least 45 days before expiry under the Central Model Rules, or earlier where state rules require it.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/rcmc-certificate-registration',
        'title' => 'RCMC Certificate: Registration, Download & Council List',
        'description' => 'What an RCMC is, which export promotion council to choose, online registration on the DGFT portal, documents, fees, validity and how to download it.',
        'lead' => 'RCMC (Registration-cum-Membership Certificate) is issued by an Export Promotion Council, commodity board or FIEO and is needed to claim benefits under India’s Foreign Trade Policy. Since 1 April 2022 it is applied for and issued online through the e-RCMC module on the DGFT portal.',
        'sections' => [
            ['RCMC full form and purpose', '<p>RCMC stands for <strong>Registration-cum-Membership Certificate</strong>. It registers an exporter with the council for its main line of business and makes it a member. Exporters need it to apply for authorisations and benefits under the Foreign Trade Policy, such as Advance Authorisation and EPCG, and to use council services such as trade fairs and market information.</p>'],
            ['Which council issues your RCMC?', '<p>There are 26 Export Promotion Councils and 9 commodity boards. Choose the one for your main products, for example:</p><ul>
<li>Engineering goods: EEPC India</li>
<li>Chemicals: CHEMEXCIL; pharmaceuticals: Pharmexcil</li>
<li>Apparel: AEPC</li>
<li>Electronics and software: ESC</li>
<li>Plastics: PLEXCONCIL</li>
<li>Gems and jewellery: GJEPC</li>
<li>Agricultural and processed food: APEDA</li>
<li>Spices: Spices Board (see <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/business-licenses/spice-board-registration">Spices Board registration</a>)</li>
</ul><p>Exporters of several unrelated products, and service exporters, can take an RCMC from FIEO; see <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/fieo-membership">FIEO membership</a>.</p>'],
            ['Certificate of origin: preferential vs non-preferential', '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Preferential</th><th>Non-preferential</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Lets the buyer claim lower or zero duty under a trade agreement</td><td>Proves where the goods were made, with no duty benefit</td></tr>
<tr><td>When needed</td><td>Exports to countries India has a trade agreement with</td><td>When the buyer, a bank or the importing country asks for proof of origin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rules</td><td>The rules of origin in the agreement</td><td>General rules of origin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Issued by</td><td>Agencies designated for each agreement</td><td>Chambers of commerce, FIEO, export councils and other notified agencies</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Both are applied for online through DGFT’s platform for certificates of origin.</p>'],
            ['How to apply for RCMC on the DGFT portal', '<ol>
<li>Make sure your <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/import-export/import-export-code-iec">IEC</a> is active and its profile updated, with a digital signature or Aadhaar e-sign linked.</li>
<li>Log in to dgft.gov.in and go to <strong>Services, e-RCMC, Apply for e-RCMC</strong>.</li>
<li>Start a fresh application and choose the council or board.</li>
<li>Enter your main line of business, products and export details.</li>
<li>Upload the documents, declare and sign.</li>
<li>Pay the council’s fee online and submit.</li>
<li>Answer any deficiency letter from the council. The issued RCMC flows into your IEC profile.</li>
</ol>'],
            ['How to download your RCMC: steps', '<ol>
<li>Log in to dgft.gov.in.</li>
<li>Go to <strong>Services, e-RCMC, View RCMC Details</strong>.</li>
<li>Open the issued certificate and download it as a PDF.</li>
</ol><p>Menu names on the DGFT portal can change; if an option has moved, use the portal’s help files.</p>'],
            ['Documents required for RCMC', '<ul>
<li>IEC, PAN and GST registration</li>
<li>Certificate of incorporation, partnership deed or other registration</li>
<li>Bank details</li>
<li>Export performance, such as shipping bills, if you have exported</li>
<li>Any other documents your council asks for</li>
</ul>'],
            ['RCMC fees', '<p>Each council sets its own membership fee, usually by category and turnover, plus GST, and it is paid online with the application. See <a href="/resources/blogs/fieo-membership-registration-fees-rcmc">FIEO membership and RCMC fees</a> for an example.</p>'],
            ['RCMC validity and renewal', '<p>An RCMC is valid for <strong>five financial years</strong>, from 1 April of the licensing year in which it is issued to 31 March of the fifth year, unless the council specifies otherwise. Renew it under <strong>Services, e-RCMC, Renewal of RCMC</strong>, and file an amendment there if your details change.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is RCMC?', 'RCMC is the Registration-cum-Membership Certificate issued by an Export Promotion Council, commodity board or FIEO. It registers an exporter with the council for its main products and is needed to claim Foreign Trade Policy benefits.'],
            ['Is RCMC mandatory for export?', 'It is not needed just to export, which only requires an IEC. It is needed to apply for authorisations and benefits under the Foreign Trade Policy, such as Advance Authorisation and EPCG, so most regular exporters take one.'],
            ['How to download an RCMC certificate?', 'Log in to the DGFT portal, go to Services, e-RCMC, View RCMC Details, open the issued certificate and download it as a PDF.'],
            ['How long is RCMC valid?', 'Five financial years, from 1 April of the licensing year in which it is issued to 31 March of the fifth year, unless the council specifies otherwise. It is renewed online on the DGFT portal.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'post',
        'key' => 'how-to-get-stqc-certification',
        'title' => 'How to Apply for STQC Certification: Step-by-Step Guide',
        'description' => 'The STQC application process step by step: choosing the scheme, lab sample submission, documents, audit, approval timeline and common rejection reasons.',
        'answer' => '<p><strong>To apply for STQC certification, choose the scheme that fits your product or system, prepare the documents, submit the application and samples to the STQC lab or certification centre, complete testing or the audit, close any findings and receive the certificate.</strong> For what STQC is and which products need it, see <a href="/certification/india/stqc-certification">STQC certification</a>; for budgeting, see <a href="/resources/blogs/stqc-certification-cost">STQC certification cost</a>.</p>',
        'top' => [
            ['Before you apply: pick the right STQC scheme', '<table><thead><tr><th>What you have</th><th>STQC route</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>CCTV cameras</td><td>Testing against MeitY’s essential security requirements for CCTV, alongside BIS registration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biometric devices for Aadhaar</td><td>STQC certification against UIDAI’s biometric device specifications</td></tr>
<tr><td>Government websites and applications</td><td>Security audit and testing before hosting or launch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Information security management system</td><td>ISO/IEC 27001 certification through STQC Certification Services</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>The scheme decides the lab, the documents and whether you need a test, an audit or both, so confirm it before you prepare anything.</p>'],
        ],
        'sections' => [
            ['STQC documents checklist', '<ul>
<li>Application form for the scheme, with the applicant’s registration documents</li>
<li>Authorisation letter for the person or representative handling the application</li>
<li>Product or system description, model list and datasheets</li>
<li>User and installation manuals</li>
<li>Hardware, firmware and software versions, and for security testing, details such as the operating system, open ports and the update process</li>
<li>For an application or website audit: the URL, test credentials and architecture details</li>
<li>Declarations and undertakings required by the scheme</li>
</ul>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/indian-registration-compliances/business-licenses/barcode-registration',
        'title' => 'Barcode Registration in India (GS1): Fees & Process',
        'description' => 'How to get genuine GS1 barcodes in India for Amazon, Flipkart and retail: fees, documents, application steps and why resold barcodes get rejected.',
        'lead' => 'Genuine product barcodes in India are issued by GS1 India; marketplaces and retailers check them against the GS1 database. Registration gives you a company prefix, and you create a GTIN for each product variant and print it as an EAN-13 barcode.',
        'sections' => [
            ['Barcodes for Amazon and Flipkart', '<p>Amazon checks product barcodes against the GS1 database, so the GTIN must be registered to your brand. Flipkart and modern retail chains usually ask for GS1 barcodes too. Brands without barcodes can sometimes apply to Amazon for a GTIN exemption, but this does not work for retail chains. Our guide to <a href="/resources/blogs/barcode-registration-india-gs1-fees-process">GS1 barcode registration</a> covers the barcode types in detail.</p>'],
            ['Why cheap barcodes get rejected', '<p>Barcodes sold cheaply online usually come from a company prefix registered to <strong>another company</strong>. When a marketplace or retailer checks the GS1 database, the brand does not match yours, and the listing can be rejected or suppressed. Large retailers also ask for proof of GS1 membership. Your own GS1 registration avoids this.</p>'],
            ['GS1 barcode fees', '<p>Fees depend on your turnover and the number of codes. For example, for a business with turnover up to ₹5 crore taking 100 codes (as of 1 October 2026):</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Registration fee</td><td>₹27,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Annual subscription</td><td>₹13,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>GST (18%)</td><td>₹7,200</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security deposit</td><td>₹3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>₹50,200</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Check GS1 India’s current fee table for your slab before applying.</p>'],
            ['Documents for GS1 registration', '<ul>
<li>Company PAN and GST registration</li>
<li>Certificate of incorporation, partnership deed or proprietorship proof</li>
<li>Proof of turnover, such as the latest ITR or a CA certificate</li>
<li>Bank details or a cancelled cheque</li>
<li>Authorised signatory details and a letter on company letterhead</li>
<li>List of products to be barcoded</li>
</ul>'],
            ['Steps to apply for GS1 barcodes', '<ol>
<li>Count every product variant and pack size that needs a code.</li>
<li>Apply online on the GS1 India website and choose the number of codes.</li>
<li>Upload the documents and pay the fees.</li>
<li>GS1 verifies the documents and allots your company prefix.</li>
<li>Create a GTIN for each product and enter its data in GS1’s system.</li>
<li>Print the barcodes on packaging following GS1 size and quality rules.</li>
</ol><p>It usually takes 7 to 10 working days after a complete application and payment. Packaged goods also need <a href="/services/indian-registration-compliances/business-licenses/lmpc-for-packaging">LMPC registration</a> for retail sale.</p>'],
            ['GS1 membership renewal', '<p>GS1 charges an annual subscription to keep your codes active. If it lapses, the codes can be flagged as inactive and marketplaces may reject them, so renew it on time every year.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['How do I get a barcode for my product in India?', 'Register with GS1 India online, upload your company and turnover documents and pay the fee. GS1 allots a company prefix, and you create a GTIN for each product variant and print it as a barcode.'],
            ['What does GS1 registration cost?', 'It depends on turnover and the number of codes. For example, 100 codes for a business with turnover up to ₹5 crore cost ₹27,000 registration plus ₹13,000 annual fee, plus GST and a ₹3,000 security deposit, about ₹50,200 in total (as of 1 October 2026).'],
            ['Are resold barcodes valid on Amazon?', 'Often not. Resold codes are usually registered to another company in the GS1 database, so Amazon can reject or suppress the listing when the brand does not match.'],
            ['Do GS1 barcodes need to be renewed?', 'Yes. GS1 charges an annual subscription to keep the codes active; if it lapses, the codes can be flagged as inactive.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'cispr-32',
        'title' => 'CISPR 32 EMC Testing: Cameras, CCTV, Audio & Video',
        'description' => 'CISPR 32 emission testing for multimedia equipment: Class A vs B limits, test setup for cameras, CCTV, audio and video products, and lab turnaround.',
        'lead' => 'CISPR 32 sets radiated and conducted emission limits for multimedia equipment such as cameras, audio and video systems and IT products. It replaced CISPR 13 and CISPR 22, and Europe uses it as EN 55032.',
        'sections' => [
            ['Class A vs Class B under CISPR 32', '<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Class A</th><th>Class B</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Intended use</td><td>Commercial and industrial settings</td><td>Homes, or anywhere close to radio and TV receivers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limits</td><td>Less strict</td><td>Stricter, usually by about 10 dB</td></tr>
<tr><td>Label or manual</td><td>Must warn that it may cause interference in a residential environment</td><td>No warning needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Servers, network switches, professional AV equipment</td><td>TVs, laptops, home cameras, soundbars</td></tr>
</tbody></table>'],
            ['CISPR 32 for CCTV and cameras', '<p>IP cameras, NVRs, DVRs and webcams fall under CISPR 32. The tests cover radiated emissions, conducted emissions on the power input, and conducted emissions on the network ports, which matters for PoE cameras. Cameras sold for homes are tested to Class B; commercial CCTV is often tested to Class A, with the warning. CCTV used in alarm and security systems also usually needs immunity testing to EN 50130-4.</p>'],
            ['CISPR 32 for audio and video systems', '<p>TVs, set-top boxes, soundbars, AV receivers, speakers and projectors are tested under CISPR 32. Products with a broadcast tuner also have tests on the antenna and tuner ports that came from the old CISPR 13. Wireless speakers and headphones need radio testing as well.</p>'],
            ['CISPR 32 test setup', '<ul>
<li><strong>Radiated emissions</strong> are measured in a semi-anechoic chamber or open-area test site, usually at 3 m or 10 m, from 30 MHz upwards; the top frequency depends on the highest internal clock frequency of the product.</li>
<li><strong>Conducted emissions</strong> are measured on AC power ports with a LISN and on wired network ports with an impedance stabilisation network, from 150 kHz to 30 MHz.</li>
<li>Table-top products are placed on a non-conductive table 0.8 m high; floor-standing products stand on a 0.1 m insulating support.</li>
<li>The product runs in typical modes with representative cables and peripherals connected. The lab’s EMC software runs a peak pre-scan, then measures quasi-peak and average values at the worst frequencies.</li>
</ul>'],
            ['CISPR 32 vs CISPR 35', '<p>CISPR 32 covers <strong>emissions</strong>: the interference a product gives off. <strong>CISPR 35</strong> covers <strong>immunity</strong>: how well it keeps working when exposed to interference such as electrostatic discharge, radiated RF fields, fast transients, surges and voltage dips. CISPR 35 replaced CISPR 20 and CISPR 24, and most multimedia products need both. Read <a href="/resources/blogs/cispr-32-emc-immunity-testing-explained">CISPR 32 and EMC immunity testing explained</a> and our <a href="/resources/blogs/emi-emc-testing-services-iec-en-61000-4">guide to EMC testing under IEC 61000-4</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is CISPR 32?', 'CISPR 32 is the international standard for radio-frequency emissions from multimedia equipment, covering IT, audio, video and broadcast receiver products. It sets limits for radiated and conducted emissions, and Europe uses it as EN 55032.'],
            ['Does CCTV need CISPR 32 testing?', 'Yes, for most markets that require EMC testing. IP cameras, NVRs and DVRs are multimedia equipment under CISPR 32, and CCTV for alarm and security systems usually also needs immunity testing to EN 50130-4.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/indian-registration-compliances/business-licenses/rni-registration',
        'title' => 'RNI Registration: Title Verification, Fees & Process',
        'description' => 'RNI registration for newspapers and magazines: full form, title verification, documents, fees, Press Sewa portal steps and certificate timeline.',
        'lead' => 'RNI (Registrar of Newspapers for India) registered newspapers and periodicals. Since 1 March 2024 its work is done by the Press Registrar General of India (PRGI), and title verification and registration are handled together online on the Press Sewa portal.',
        'sections' => [
            ['RNI full form and the move to PRGI', '<p>RNI stands for <strong>Registrar of Newspapers for India</strong>. It registered newspapers and periodicals under the Press and Registration of Books Act, 1867. The <strong>Press and Registration of Periodicals Act, 2023</strong> replaced that Act from 1 March 2024, and the office became the Press Registrar General of India (PRGI). Title verification and registration, which used to be separate steps, now happen in one online application on the Press Sewa portal.</p>'],
            ['Title verification', '<p>You suggest one or more titles in order of preference. A title must not be the same as, or similar to, a title already held by another periodical in the same language anywhere in India, or in any language in the same state, and it must follow PRGI’s title guidelines. PRGI checks the titles as part of the registration application, so choose distinctive options.</p>'],
            ['Documents for periodical registration', '<ul>
<li>Details and ID of the owner and the publisher, with the owner’s authorisation of the publisher</li>
<li>Proposed titles, language, periodicity and place of publication</li>
<li>Details of the editor and the printing press</li>
<li>Declarations in the formats provided on the Press Sewa portal</li>
<li>For a company or society: registration documents and a board resolution</li>
</ul><p>The printer must separately inform PRGI and the district authority on the Press Sewa portal within 30 days of starting a printing press.</p>'],
            ['RNI registration fees', '<table><thead><tr><th>Application</th><th>Fee</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Certificate of registration for a new periodical</td><td>₹1,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Revision of particulars, such as title, language, periodicity or place</td><td>₹1,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transfer of ownership</td><td>₹1,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Facsimile edition of a foreign periodical</td><td>₹10,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Fees are paid online through Bharat Kosh, as set by the Press and Registration of Periodicals Rules, 2024.</p>'],
            ['Registration steps on the Press Sewa portal', '<ol>
<li>Create an account on the Press Sewa portal as the owner, and authorise the publisher.</li>
<li>Fill in the application with the titles, language, periodicity, place of publication, editor and printing press details.</li>
<li>Upload the documents and pay the ₹1,000 fee. You get an application reference number.</li>
<li>Correct any mistakes within the <strong>five-day</strong> window after submission; no changes are possible after that.</li>
<li>The application goes to PRGI and to the Specified Authority, usually the District Magistrate, who has <strong>60 days</strong> to give comments.</li>
<li>PRGI raises any deficiencies on the portal, which you must fix within 30 days.</li>
<li>PRGI issues the certificate of registration, and you can start publishing.</li>
</ol>'],
            ['How long RNI registration takes: timeline', '<p>The Specified Authority has up to 60 days to comment, and PRGI flags deficiencies within about seven working days of receiving the application. A complete application with a clearly distinct title is usually registered within two to three months; deficiencies or title objections add time.</p>'],
            ['Validity and annual statement', '<p>The certificate of registration does not have to be renewed. The publisher must file an <strong>annual statement</strong> on the Press Sewa portal for each financial year; failing to do so within one year of the end of that year can lead to a penalty. Changes to the title, language, periodicity, place, editor or printing press need a revision application.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is RNI registration?', 'It is the registration of a newspaper or periodical with the Press Registrar General of India (formerly the Registrar of Newspapers for India). Title verification and registration are now done together online on the Press Sewa portal.'],
            ['What are RNI registration fees?', 'The government fee for a certificate of registration of a new periodical is ₹1,000, paid through Bharat Kosh on the Press Sewa portal. Revisions and transfers of ownership are also ₹1,000 each.'],
            ['How long does RNI registration take?', 'Usually two to three months for a complete application. The District Magistrate or other Specified Authority has 60 days to comment, and any deficiencies must be fixed within 30 days.'],
        ],
    ],

    [
        'type' => 'service',
        'key' => 'services/testing/reach-testing',
        'title' => 'REACH & SVHC Testing Laboratory: Scope, Cost & Turnaround',
        'description' => 'REACH SVHC testing through ISO/IEC 17025-accredited partner labs: what is tested, the current SVHC list, sample needs, turnaround, cost and report format.',
        'lead' => 'We arrange REACH SVHC and Annex XVII testing at ISO/IEC 17025-accredited partner labs. The report shows whether any substance of very high concern is above 0.1% by weight and whether the restrictions that apply to your product are met.',
        'sections' => [
            ['What REACH testing covers', '<ul>
<li><strong>SVHC screening:</strong> testing against ECHA’s Candidate List of substances of very high concern, which is updated about twice a year. If an article contains an SVHC above 0.1% by weight, EU suppliers must tell their customers and notify ECHA’s SCIP database, and EU producers and importers above one tonne a year must also notify ECHA.</li>
<li><strong>Annex XVII restrictions:</strong> limits on specific substances in specific products, such as azo dyes in textiles and leather, nickel release from items in contact with skin, PAHs in rubber and plastic parts, phthalates in articles, lead in consumer articles, cadmium, and hexavalent chromium in leather.</li>
</ul><p>We choose the tests for your product and target market, so you do not pay for tests that do not apply.</p>'],
            ['REACH for exporters: which products', '<p>REACH applies to products sold in the EU, and EU buyers usually ask for test reports for:</p><ul>
<li>textiles, garments and footwear</li>
<li>leather goods</li>
<li>toys and children’s products</li>
<li>jewellery and accessories</li>
<li>plastic and rubber products</li>
<li>electrical and electronic products, usually tested together with RoHS</li>
<li>furniture and home goods</li>
</ul><p>Read our <a href="/resources/blogs/reach-svhc-testing-compliance-guide-exporters">REACH SVHC testing guide for exporters</a>.</p>'],
            ['Sample requirements', '<p>Send finished products or the materials they are made from. The lab tests each <strong>homogeneous material</strong>, such as a fabric, a coating, a plastic part or a metal component, so include every material, colour and component you want covered. The lab confirms the quantity needed for the tests chosen. Add a bill of materials and any supplier declarations; they help target the testing.</p>'],
            ['Turnaround and cost', '<p>Standard turnaround is usually <strong>5 to 10 working days</strong> after the lab receives the samples, with express options at some labs. Cost depends on:</p><ul>
<li>full SVHC screening or targeted tests;</li>
<li>the number of homogeneous materials; and</li>
<li>the Annex XVII restrictions that apply to your product.</li>
</ul><p>Grouping materials that share a supplier and composition reduces the number of tests.</p>'],
            ['What the test report shows', '<ul>
<li>Sample description and photos, with the materials tested</li>
<li>The version of the Candidate List and the restrictions tested</li>
<li>Test methods and detection limits</li>
<li>Results for each material, with any SVHC found and its concentration</li>
<li>A pass or fail against each restriction</li>
</ul><p>Products that are chemicals or mixtures also need a safety data sheet; see our <a href="/certification/global-certifications/msds-authoring">MSDS authoring service</a>.</p>'],
        ],
        'faqs' => [
            ['What is REACH testing?', 'It is laboratory testing of a product against the EU REACH regulation: screening for substances of very high concern on ECHA’s Candidate List, and checking the Annex XVII restrictions that apply to the product, such as azo dyes, nickel, PAHs or phthalates.'],
            ['How much does SVHC testing cost?', 'It depends on whether you need full SVHC screening or targeted tests, and on how many homogeneous materials are tested. Grouping similar materials keeps the cost down, and we quote after reviewing the product.'],
            ['How long does REACH testing take?', 'Usually 5 to 10 working days after the lab receives the samples. Express testing is available at some labs.'],
        ],
    ],

